\import{fcap-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\tag{fcap}
\tag{spin}
\tag{representation-theory}

\taxon{Proposition}
\title{The central sign determines descent to the special orthogonal group}

\p{Keep the hypotheses and notation of \ref{fcap-003L}, and let
#{q:G\to SO(n)} be the vector action. The integrated representation
#{R} factors through a smooth representation #{\overline R} of
#{SO(n)} precisely when

##{R(-1)=\operatorname{Id}_S.}

Equivalently, let #{J\in\mathfrak{so}(n)} send
#{e_1\mapsto-e_2}, #{e_2\mapsto e_1}, and vanish on the remaining
coordinate vectors. Then the descent condition is

##{\exp\bigl(2\pi r(J)\bigr)=\operatorname{Id}_S.}}

\subtree{
\taxon{Proof}
\p{The covering \ref{fcap-0039} has kernel #{\{1,-1\}}. A
factorization forces #{R(-1)=\overline R(1)=\operatorname{Id}_S}.
Conversely, under that condition set
#{\overline R(q(g))=R(g)}. Any two lifts differ by #{1} or #{-1},
so the value is independent of the lift. Surjectivity of #{q} proves
both the homomorphism law and uniqueness of #{\overline R}. The
differential of #{q} is an isomorphism by \ref{fcap-0037}; its local
smooth inverse sections express #{\overline R} locally as #{R}
composed with a smooth section. Hence the descended representation is
smooth. Finally, \ref{fcap-0038} gives

##{\exp\bigl(2\pi b(J)\bigr)=-1,}

so exponential naturality identifies #{R(-1)} with the displayed
matrix exponential.}
}

\p{This criterion compares the integrated action with Clifford modules.
Suppose

##{f:\Cl^0(Q_n^+)\longrightarrow\operatorname{End}_F(S)}

is a unital real-algebra homomorphism from the even Clifford algebra.
Its restriction #{R_f(g)=f(g)} takes Spin to invertible operators,
with inverse #{f(g^{-1})}. Finite-dimensional linearity makes this
restriction smooth. Since #{b(A)} is even, the exponential power series
stays inside #{\Cl^0(Q_n^+)} and gives

##{R_f\bigl(\exp(t b(A))\bigr)
=\exp\bigl(t f(b(A))\bigr).}

Since #{f} preserves commutators, #{r(A)=f(b(A))} is a Lie-algebra
action. Consequently #{d(R_f)_1(b(A))=f(b(A))}. The uniqueness in
\ref{fcap-003L} identifies #{R_f} with the integrated action for
#{r(A)=f(b(A))}; it is an equality on the chosen module, not merely
an unspecified representation equivalence.}

\p{Unitality gives #{R_f(-1)=-\operatorname{Id}_S}. Thus a nonzero
such module does not descend to #{SO(n)}. This includes the complex
spin and half-spin modules: they are restrictions of modules for the
complexified even Clifford algebra, as described in
\citet{Section 3.7.5, pp. 80--81}{meinrenken2013clifford}.
No extension of a half-spin module to the full Clifford algebra is needed.
The central scalar #{-1} is fixed by the even-algebra sign comparison in
\ref{fcap-0039}, so this obstruction is unchanged by the two Clifford
sign conventions.}

\p{The same calculation shows why tensor constructions can descend.
On #{S\otimes_F S} the central element acts by
#{(-\operatorname{Id})\otimes(-\operatorname{Id})=\operatorname{Id}};
on #{\operatorname{End}_F(S)} its conjugation action is also the identity.
Both actions therefore factor through #{SO(n)}. Differentiating their
defining group actions gives, respectively,

##{\begin{aligned}
A\cdot(s\otimes t)&=r(A)s\otimes t+s\otimes r(A)t,\\
A\cdot T&=r(A)T-Tr(A).
\end{aligned}}

The tensor formula agrees with
\citet{Section 8.1, equation (8.12), p. 110}{fulton2004representation}.
Descent concerns the central action of the whole group, even though these
differentiated formulas use only its Lie algebra.}

\p{For an action of the full real Clifford algebra, TauCeti's
\leanref/tauceti{AlgHom.lieMap_realCliffordSpinSmoothHom_eq}
identifies its differentiated smooth restriction with the quadratic
Clifford action. The declaration uses the closed Spin range in Clifford
units and its Lie-algebra coordinates; the natural carrier equivalence
transports the action back to #{G}. Its input is a full-Clifford algebra
homomorphism into a finite-dimensional normed real algebra. It does not
state the even-module extension or the descent criterion proved here.}
